DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261028
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Standing Order: Standing Order Requiring Pape
 r Filing for Election Day 2026 Emergency Matters
DESCRIPTION:STATE OF MAINE       SUPERIOR COURT \nDISTRICT COURT \n \n  \n
 STANDING ORDER REQUIRING \nPAPER FILING FOR ELECTION DAY \n2026 EMERGENCY 
 MATTERS \n \n \n The Maine Judicial Branch (MJB) is implementing the Maine
  eCourts \nsystem – including eFiling – statewide.1  EFileMaine is a p
 ractical and efficient \ndocument submission system already in use in many
  courts throughout the \nstate.  However\, because filings related to Elec
 tion Day may be uniquely time-\nsensitive\, to ensure timely processing\, 
 the MJB will require paper filing under \nthe limited circumstances descri
 bed below.   \n \n It is therefore ORDERED that any emergency Title 21-A f
 ilings related to \nElection Day 2026 filed between 4:00 p.m. on Friday\, 
 October 30\, 2026\, and \n9:00 p.m. on Tuesday\, November 3\, 2026\, must 
 be by paper\, unless otherwise \nordered.2 \n \nIt is further ORDERED that
  the Maine Rules of Electronic Court Systems \nare SUSPENDED to the extent
  necessary to give effect to this order.   \n \n \n \nDated: 10/28/2026   
 _____/s/______________________   \n       Chief Justice Robert E. Mullen \
 n \n \n \nDated: 10/28/2026   ___/s/_______________________   \n       Chi
 ef Judge Brent Davis \n \n1 Further information about the Maine eCourts sy
 stem is available at \nhttps://www.courts.maine.gov/ecourts/index.html \n2
  Any Election Day 2026 filings filed by eFile during this period may not b
 e processed until the next business \nday after the election.\nhttps://www
 .courts.maine.gov/adminorders/so-election-day-procedure.pdf
URL:https://www.courts.maine.gov/adminorders/so-election-day-procedure.pdf
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:e6b9db315a893cfb@role-real-estate-title.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261123T172323Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261119
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] State of Maine v. Ryan P. Reardon
DESCRIPTION:MAINE SUPREME JUDICIAL COURT Reporter of Decisions \nDecision:
   2026 ME 109 \nDocket: Pis-25-93 \nArgued:  December 10\, 2025 \nDecided:
   November 19\, 2026 \n \nPanel:  STANFILL\, C.J.\, and MEAD\, CONNORS\, L
 AWRENCE\, DOUGLAS\, and LIPEZ\, JJ. \n \n \nSTATE OF MAINE \n \nv. \n \nRY
 AN P. REARDON \n \nLIPEZ\, J. \n[¶1]  In this appeal\, Ryan P. Reardon as
 ks us to determine what options \nare available to a trial court when the 
 court finds that a defendant\, for reasons \noutside his control\, has not
  complied with deferment requirements  imposed \nunder 17-A M.R.S. § 1902
 (1) (2026).  Pursuant to an agreement with the State\, \nReardon pleaded g
 uilty to domestic violence assault on the condition that if he \nsuccessfu
 lly complied with the deferment requirements \, then he would be \npermitt
 ed to withdraw his guilty plea and instead plead guilty to  a charge of  \
 ndomestic violence reckless conduct.  At a final hearing following the def
 erment \nperiod\, the trial court (Piscataquis County\, Larson\, J.) found
  that Reardon had \nfailed to comply with certain deferment requirements b
 ut that his failure to \ncomply was excusable.  The court nonetheless conc
 luded\, relying on principles \nof contract interpretation\,  that Reardon
  was not entitled to the successful \n 2 \noutcome of the agreement and ac
 cordingly entered a judgment of conviction for \ndomestic violence assault
 . \n[¶2]  On appeal\, Reardon does not dispute his lack of compliance.  I
 nstead\, \nhe argues that the trial court \, upon finding that his noncomp
 liance was \nexcusable\, should have allowed him to withdraw his guilty pl
 ea to domestic \nviolence assault and plead guilty instead to reckless con
 duct.  We do not reach \nthe question of the propriety of the court’s ac
 tion\, however\, because we \nconclude that we do not have jurisdiction to
  entertain Reardon’s appeal.  Title \n17-A M.R.S. § 1904 (2026)  prohib
 its “attack[s]” on the “final disposition” of a \ndeferred disposi
 tion except in cases where a court has sentenced a person upon \nfinding t
 hat the person’s fail\nhttps://www.courts.maine.gov/courts/sjc/lawcourt/
 2026/26me109.pdf
URL:https://www.courts.maine.gov/courts/sjc/lawcourt/2026/26me109.pdf
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:35f7402c4b434042@role-real-estate-title.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261027T175726Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261027
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] State of Maine v. Devon M. Stewart
DESCRIPTION:MAINE SUPREME JUDICIAL COURT Reporter of Decisions \nDecision:
   2026 ME 104 \nDocket: Som-25-305 \nArgued: March 5\, 2026 \nDecided:  Oc
 tober 27\, 2026 \n \nPanel:  STANFILL\, C.J.\, and MEAD\, CONNORS\, LAWREN
 CE\, DOUGLAS\, and LIPEZ\, JJ.  \n \n \nSTATE OF MAINE \n \nv. \n \nDEVON 
 M. STEWART \n \n \nLAWRENCE\, J. \n[¶1]  Devon M. Stewart appeals from a 
 judgment of conviction of one \ncount of gross sexual assault  entered by 
 the trial court (Somerset County\, \nMullen\, C.J.) after a jury trial.  S
 tewart challenges the sufficiency of the evidence\, \nargues that the tria
 l court erred by admitting in evidence the victim’s 9-1-1 call \nand her
  testimony regarding Stewart’s out-of-court statement\, and asserts that
  \nthe trial court abused its discretion  during the second step of the se
 ntencing \nanalysis by improperly setting his probation for longer than  w
 hat is allowed \nunder 17-A M.R.S. § 1804(1)(A) (2025).1  We conclude tha
 t there was sufficient \nevidence to support a conviction of gross sexual 
 assault by compulsion\, that \n \n1  Title 17-A M.R.S. § 1804(1) has sinc
 e been amended but not in any way that affects this appeal.  \nSee P.L. 20
 25\, ch. 207\, § 2 (effective Sep. 24\, 2025) (codified at 17-A M.R.S. §
  1804(1) (2026)). \n 2 \nStewart waived his argument regarding the 9 -1-1 
 call\, and that the trial court \ndid not err in admitti ng the victim’s
  testimony regarding Stewart’s statement.  \nHowever\, we also conclude 
 that the trial court erred by improperly sentencing \nStewart to a longer 
 probationary period than is allowed by statute. 2  We \ntherefore affirm t
 he conviction but vacate the sentence and remand for \nresentencing. \nI. 
  BACKGROUND \n[¶2]  Viewing the evidence in the light most favorable to t
 he verdict\, the \njury rationally could have found the following facts be
 yond a reasonable doubt.  \nSee State v. Hansen\, 2020 ME 43\, ¶ 2\, 228 
 A.3d 1082. \n [¶3]  On February 17\, 2023\, the sixteen -year-old victim 
 was walking in \nWaterville toward Fairfield.  The victim recognized Stewa
 rt\, a former \nschoolmat\nhttps://www.courts.maine.gov/courts/sjc/lawcour
 t/2026/26me104.pdf
URL:https://www.courts.maine.gov/courts/sjc/lawcourt/2026/26me104.pdf
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
